# Lesson 1: Finding and switching files

Goal: never reach for the mouse or `:e path/to/file` again.
First, open the practice project:  `:Practice`{vim}
It opens in a new tab. Read the steps here, try them there. `]t`{normal} goes to
the project, `[t`{normal} comes back here.

# 1.1 Find a file by name

`<Space>ff`{normal}  Find files

 1. In the practice tab, press `<Space>ff`{normal}.
 2. Type `mon`. Fuzzy matching finds money.ts from any part of the name.
 3. Press `<Enter>`{normal} to open it.

Inside any picker:
  `<C-j>`{normal} / `<C-k>`{normal}   move down / up the list
  `<C-v>`{normal}             open the result in a vertical split
  `<C-s>`{normal}             open it in a horizontal split
  `<C-t>`{normal}             open it in a new tab
  `<A-p>`{normal}             hide / show the preview
  `<Esc>`{normal}             close the picker

# 1.2 Find text anywhere in the project

`<Space>fw`{normal}  Find words (live search across files)

 1. Press `<Space>fw`{normal} and type `addMoney`.
 2. You'll see where it's defined (money.ts) and used (cart.ts).
 3. Move to the cart.ts line and press `<Enter>`{normal}. You land ON that line.

`<Space>fW`{normal} is the same but also searches files git ignores.
`<Space>f<Enter>`{normal} reopens your last search exactly as you left it.

# 1.3 Switch between files you've opened

Every opened file is a BUFFER, shown in the bar along the top.

  `]b`{normal} / `[b`{normal}     next / previous buffer
  `<Space>fs`{normal}     buffers, recent files and project files in one picker
  `<Space>fb`{normal}     just the open buffers
  `<Space>fo`{normal}     recently opened files (across projects)
  `<Space>bb`{normal}     pick a buffer from the top bar by a letter
  `<Space>c`{normal}      close this buffer
  `<Space>bc`{normal}     close every buffer except this one

 1. Open cart.ts, money.ts and index.ts (use `<Space>ff`{normal} three times).
 2. Cycle through them with `]b`{normal} and `[b`{normal}.
 3. Press `<Space>bb`{normal}: each tab in the top bar gets a letter. Press one.
 4. Close money.ts with `<Space>c`{normal} while it's showing.

`<Space>fs`{normal} is the one to make a habit: one picker for "that file I was
just in" and "that file I haven't opened yet".

# 1.4 The file explorer

  `<Space>e`{normal}   show / hide the explorer (left side)
  `<Space>o`{normal}   jump the cursor into it (or back)

Inside the explorer:
  `<Enter>`{normal}   open file / expand folder    `a`{normal}  add a file (end with / for a folder)
  `s`{normal}         open in a vertical split     `r`{normal}  rename
  `S`{normal}         open in a horizontal split   `d`{normal}  delete
  `H`{normal}         show hidden files            `?`{normal}  all the explorer's keys

 1. `<Space>e`{normal}, move to src/ and press `a`{normal}. Type `notes.md` and `<Enter>`{normal}.
 2. Rename it with `r`{normal} to `todo.md`, then delete it with `d`{normal}.
 3. `<Space>e`{normal} again to hide the explorer.

# 1.5 Harpoon: pin the files you're working on

Most of the time you bounce between 3-4 files. Pin them.

  `<Space><Space>a`{normal}   pin the current file
  `<Space><Space>e`{normal}   show pinned files (reorder: move lines; remove: `dd`)
  `<C-n>`{normal} / `<C-p>`{normal}       next / previous pinned file
  `<C-x>`{normal}             jump to pin number N (it asks for the number)

 1. Pin cart.ts, then money.ts, then index.ts (`<Space><Space>a`{normal} in each).
 2. Press `<C-p>`{normal} and `<C-n>`{normal} to hop between just those three.
 3. `<C-x>`{normal} then `1`{normal} `<Enter>`{normal} jumps straight to cart.ts.
 4. `<Space><Space>e`{normal} shows the list. Reorder it with `dd`{normal} and `p`{normal}, then `<Esc>`{normal}.

Pins are saved per project, so they're still there tomorrow.

# 1.6 Save and new files

  `<Space>w`{normal}   save          `<Space>n`{normal}   new empty file

# Recap

  find by name     `<Space>ff`          find text      `<Space>fw`
  anything recent  `<Space>fs`          resume search  `<Space>f<Enter>`
  next buffer      `]b`                 close buffer   `<Space>c`
  explorer         `<Space>e`           pin file       `<Space><Space>a`
  hop pins         `<C-n>` / `<C-p>`

Next: `:Tutor my-02-windows`{vim}
